(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["prefix",{"l:prefix/prefix-sums-and-half-open-ranges":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Store \u003ccode>pre[i]\u003c/code> as the sum of the first i elements, so \u003ccode>pre[0] = 0\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>pre\u003c/code> has n + 1 entries. The sum of the half-open slice \u003ccode>a[l:r]\u003c/code> is then \u003ccode>pre[r] - pre[l]\u003c/code>: one subtraction, whatever the length. You pay O(n) once to build the table and O(1) per query, as long as the data does not change.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Invariant:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>pre[i] == sum(a[:i])\u003c/code>. It holds for i = 0 (the empty sum) and \u003ccode>pre[i + 1] = pre[i] + a[i]\u003c/code> keeps it. Subtracting two prefixes cancels their shared part, so \u003ccode>pre[r] - pre[l]\u003c/code> is exactly \u003ccode>a[l] + ... + a[r - 1]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Why half-open:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>a[l:r]\u003c/code> matches Python slicing and has length \u003ccode>r - l\u003c/code>, and the leading zero removes the special case \u003ccode>l == 0\u003c/code>. An inclusive question \u003ccode>[l, r]\u003c/code> becomes \u003ccode>pre[r + 1] - pre[l]\u003c/code>; convert once at the edge of your code, never in the middle.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fence posts:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>pre[i]\u003c/code> sits on the post just before \u003ccode>a[i]\u003c/code>, which is why there is one more post than there are panels.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Python costs:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>list(accumulate(a, initial=0))\u003c/code> runs the loop in C, several times faster than a Python \u003ccode>for\u003c/code> loop. Each prefix is its own int object (28 bytes or more, plus an 8-byte list slot); for millions of numbers \u003ccode>numpy.cumsum\u003c/code> stores packed 8-byte integers instead. Python ints never overflow; NumPy's int64 and Java's or C++'s fixed-width integers do, silently.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The hidden O(n):\u003c/b> \u003ccode>sum(a[l:r])\u003c/code> inside a loop copies the slice and adds it up every time. A loop of q such queries is O(nq), exactly the cost the table removes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Static data only:\u003c/b> changing \u003ccode>a[i]\u003c/code> invalidates every entry from \u003ccode>pre[i + 1]\u003c/code> on, an O(n) rewrite. When updates and queries interleave, a Fenwick tree keeps both at O(log n); the range-query module builds one.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from itertools import accumulate\n\n\ndef build_prefix(a):\n    \"\"\"pre[i] = a[0] + ... + a[i-1], so pre[0] = 0 and len(pre) = len(a) + 1.\"\"\"\n    pre = [0] * (len(a) + 1)\n    for i, x in enumerate(a):\n        pre[i + 1] = pre[i] + x\n    return pre\n\n\ndef range_sum(pre, l, r):\n    \"\"\"Sum of a[l:r] (half-open), O(1).\"\"\"\n    return pre[r] - pre[l]\n\n\n# The same table in one line, with the loop running in C:\n# pre = list(accumulate(a, initial=0))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start with \u003ccode>pre[0] = 0\u003c/code>, the sum of the first zero elements; \u003ccode>pre\u003c/code> will have one more entry than \u003ccode>a\u003c/code>\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0   1   2   3   4   5   6   7\na         3   1   4   1   5   9   2\npre       \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">\u003ccode>pre[i + 1] = pre[i] + a[i]\u003c/code>: 0 + 3 = 3, then 3 + 1 = 4, then 4 + 4 = 8\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0   1   2   3   4   5   6   7\na         3   1   4   1   5   9   2\npre       0   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Carry on to the end: \u003ccode>pre[7] = 25\u003c/code> is the total. Invariant: \u003ccode>pre[i]\u003c/code> is the sum of the \u003ccode>a\u003c/code> values in columns before i\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0   1   2   3   4   5   6   7\na         3   1   4   1   5   9   2\npre       0   3   4   8   \u003cmark>9\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>14\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>23\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>25\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Query \u003ccode>sum(a[2:5])\u003c/code>, the 4, 1 and 5: it is everything before column 5 minus everything before column 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0   1   2   3   4   5   6   7\na         3   1   \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>   9   2\npre       0   3   \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>   8   9  \u003cmark>14\u003c/mark>  23  25\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">\u003ccode>pre[5] - pre[2] = 14 - 4 = 10\u003c/code>: two lookups, whatever the length of the range\u003c/p>\u003cpre>sum(a[2:5]) = 14 - 4 = 10 = 4 + 1 + 5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Mixed conventions:\u003c/b> answering an inclusive \u003ccode>[l, r]\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>pre[r] - pre[l]\u003c/code> silently drops \u003ccode>a[r]\u003c/code>. Write the convention in the docstring.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>No leading zero:\u003c/b> building \u003ccode>pre[i] = sum(a[:i + 1])\u003c/code> forces an \u003ccode>if l == 0\u003c/code> branch and invites off-by-one errors.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Stale tables:\u003c/b> mutating \u003ccode>a\u003c/code> after building \u003ccode>pre\u003c/code> does not update it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Floats:\u003c/b> differences of large running totals lose precision (cancellation). Keep integers (cents, counts), or use \u003ccode>math.fsum\u003c/code> when exactness matters.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Overflow elsewhere:\u003c/b> 2 x 10^5 values up to 10^9 sum to 2 x 10^14, beyond a 32-bit \u003ccode>int\u003c/code>; use \u003ccode>long long\u003c/code> in C++ and \u003ccode>long\u003c/code> in Java.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">For \u003ccode>a = [2, -1, 3, 0, 4]\u003c/code>, what is \u003ccode>pre\u003c/code>, and which two entries give the sum of \u003ccode>a[1:4]\u003c/code>?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>pre = [0, 2, 1, 4, 4, 8]\u003c/code>. The sum of \u003ccode>a[1:4]\u003c/code> (that is -1, 3 and 0) is \u003ccode>pre[4] - pre[1] = 4 - 2 = 2\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to turn any static range-sum question into two lookups, and why the leading zero and half-open ranges remove the off-by-one errors.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:prefix/counting-subarrays-with-a-prefix-map":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>The subarray \u003ccode>a[j:i]\u003c/code> sums to k exactly when \u003ccode>pre[i] - pre[j] == k\u003c/code>, that is when some \u003cb>earlier prefix equals \u003ccode>cur - k\u003c/code>\u003c/b>. Scan once with a running sum \u003ccode>cur\u003c/code> and a map from every prefix value seen so far to how often it occurred: add \u003ccode>seen[cur - k]\u003c/code> to the answer, then record \u003ccode>cur\u003c/code>. That is O(n) time and O(n) space, and negative values change nothing.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Why not a sliding window:\u003c/b> a window shrinks when its sum is too big, which assumes that adding an element never lowers the sum. One negative value breaks that assumption and the window skips valid answers. Prefix differences do not care about signs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Look up, then record:\u003c/b> after adding \u003ccode>a[i]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>cur\u003c/code> is \u003ccode>pre[i + 1]\u003c/code> and the map holds \u003ccode>pre[0..i]\u003c/code>, so every match is a non-empty subarray ending at i. Recording \u003ccode>cur\u003c/code> first lets it match itself: an empty subarray, which sums to 0 and corrupts the answer when k = 0.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Seed \u003ccode>{0: 1}\u003c/code>:\u003c/b> the empty prefix \u003ccode>pre[0] = 0\u003c/code> is what lets subarrays starting at index 0 be counted.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Counter, defaultdict or dict.get:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>Counter\u003c/code> returns 0 for a missing key \u003ci>without inserting it\u003c/i>; \u003ccode>defaultdict(int)\u003c/code> inserts on every read, so failed lookups grow the map; \u003ccode>seen.get(x, 0)\u003c/code> works on a plain dict.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>One scan, several questions:\u003c/b> to count, map prefixes to counts; for the longest, map each prefix to its \u003ci>first\u003c/i> index (seed \u003ccode>{0: -1}\u003c/code>, never overwrite); for the shortest, keep the \u003ci>last\u003c/i> index (overwrite); to test existence, a set is enough.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cost:\u003c/b> n lookups and n inserts at expected O(1) each (the hashing module covers why), plus O(n) space for the distinct prefixes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Two dimensions:\u003c/b> fix a band of rows, collapse each column of the band to its sum, and run this count on that 1D array (the 2D recap shows the band trick).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import Counter\n\n\ndef count_sum_k(a, k):\n    \"\"\"Number of subarrays with sum exactly k, O(n).\"\"\"\n    seen = Counter({0: 1})         # the empty prefix, before a[0]\n    cur = count = 0\n    for x in a:\n        cur += x\n        count += seen[cur - k]     # earlier prefixes p with cur - p == k\n        seen[cur] += 1\n    return count\n\n\ndef longest_sum_k(a, k):\n    \"\"\"Length of the longest subarray with sum exactly k (0 if none), O(n).\"\"\"\n    first = {0: -1}                # prefix value -&gt; first index where it occurred\n    cur = best = 0\n    for i, x in enumerate(a):\n        cur += x\n        if cur - k in first:\n            best = max(best, i - first[cur - k])\n        first.setdefault(cur, i)   # keep the earliest index only\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count the subarrays of \u003ccode>[3, 4, -7, 3, 1, 3]\u003c/code> that sum to k = 7. Watch prefix 0 appear twice: that duplicate is why the map stores counts, not just presence.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Seed the map with the empty prefix: \u003ccode>seen = {0: 1}\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>cur = 0\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>count = 0\u003c/code>\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         3   4  -7   3   1   3\nseen  {0: 1}\ncount 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 0: \u003ccode>cur = 3\u003c/code>, look up \u003ccode>cur - k = -4\u003c/code>: not seen, so no subarray ends here; then record 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   4  -7   3   1   3\ncur   3    need -4    matches 0\nseen  {0: 1, 3: 1}\ncount 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 1: \u003ccode>cur = 7\u003c/code>, look up \u003ccode>0\u003c/code>: seen once (the empty prefix), so \u003ccode>a[0:2] = [3, 4]\u003c/code> sums to 7\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         3   \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>  -7   3   1   3\ncur   7    need 0    matches 1\nseen  {0: 1, 3: 1, 7: 1}\ncount 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 2: \u003ccode>cur = 0\u003c/code>, look up \u003ccode>cur - k = -7\u003c/code>: not seen, so no subarray ends here; then record 0, which is now in the map twice\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         3   4  \u003cmark>-7\u003c/mark>   3   1   3\ncur   0    need -7    matches 0\nseen  {0: 2, 3: 1, 7: 1}\ncount 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 3: \u003ccode>cur = 3\u003c/code>, look up \u003ccode>cur - k = -4\u003c/code>: not seen, so no subarray ends here; then record 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         3   4  -7   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   1   3\ncur   3    need -4    matches 0\nseen  {0: 2, 3: 2, 7: 1}\ncount 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 4: \u003ccode>cur = 4\u003c/code>, look up \u003ccode>cur - k = -3\u003c/code>: not seen, so no subarray ends here; then record 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         3   4  -7   3   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   3\ncur   4    need -3    matches 0\nseen  {0: 2, 3: 2, 7: 1, 4: 1}\ncount 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 5: \u003ccode>cur = 7\u003c/code>, look up \u003ccode>0\u003c/code>: seen 2 times, the empty prefix and the prefix after i = 2, so \u003ccode>a[0:6]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>a[3:6] = [3, 1, 3]\u003c/code> both sum to 7\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         3   4  -7   3   1   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\ncur   7    need 0    matches 2\nseen  {0: 2, 3: 2, 7: 2, 4: 1}\ncount 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Recording before looking up:\u003c/b> counts empty subarrays whenever k = 0.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>No seed:\u003c/b> misses every subarray that starts at index 0; for the longest variant, seeding \u003ccode>{0: 0}\u003c/code> instead of \u003ccode>{0: -1}\u003c/code> makes those lengths one too short.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Overwriting first indices\u003c/b> in the longest variant shortens answers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Using it where a window is better:\u003c/b> with non-negative values and a \"sum at most k\" condition, a sliding window needs O(1) space. The map is for exact targets and signed data.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \u003ccode>count_sum_k([0, 0, 0], 0)\u003c/code> return, and what would it return if the loop recorded \u003ccode>cur\u003c/code> before the lookup?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>6, one for each of the 3 + 2 + 1 non-empty subarrays. Recording first lets each step also match itself (an empty subarray ending there), adding 3 more: 9.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to count or measure subarrays with an exact sum in one pass by asking the map how many earlier prefixes equal \u003ccode>cur - k\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:prefix/remainders-xor-and-balance-as-prefix-keys":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Many subarray conditions depend only on a \u003cb>key\u003c/b> of the two bounding prefixes: equal remainders mod k mean the sum is divisible by k, prefix XORs that differ by k (in XOR terms) mean the subarray's XOR is k, and equal balances (with 0 scored as -1) mean equal counts. Run the same one-pass scan as the prefix map, but store the key instead of the raw sum.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Question about \u003ccode>a[j:i]\u003c/code>\u003c/th>\u003cth>Key per prefix\u003c/th>\u003cth>Look up\u003c/th>\u003cth>Map value\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sum divisible by k\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>pre % k\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>the same remainder\u003c/td>\u003ctd>count\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sum a multiple of k, length at least 2\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>pre % k\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>the same remainder, two or more positions back\u003c/td>\u003ctd>first index\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>XOR equals k\u003c/td>\u003ctd>prefix XOR\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>cur ^ k\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>count\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>As many 0s as 1s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>sum with 0 scored as -1\u003c/td>\u003ctd>the same balance\u003c/td>\u003ctd>first index\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Every vowel an even number of times\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5-bit parity mask\u003c/td>\u003ctd>the same mask\u003c/td>\u003ctd>first index\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Why remainders work:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>(pre[i] - pre[j]) % k == 0\u003c/code> exactly when \u003ccode>pre[i] % k == pre[j] % k\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>XOR is its own inverse:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>px[i] ^ px[j]\u003c/code> is the XOR of \u003ccode>a[j:i]\u003c/code>, so the XOR is k exactly when \u003ccode>px[j] == px[i] ^ k\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Negative remainders:\u003c/b> Python's \u003ccode>%\u003c/code> takes the sign of the divisor, so \u003ccode>-5 % 3 == 1\u003c/code>. C++ and Java truncate towards zero (\u003ccode>-5 % 3 == -2\u003c/code>); normalise with \u003ccode>((x % k) + k) % k\u003c/code> or equal remainders will not match.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Small key spaces:\u003c/b> with keys in \u003ccode>0..k-1\u003c/code> (or 32 parity masks), a list of size k replaces the dict: O(k) space and no hashing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Counting pairs at the end:\u003c/b> a key seen c times contributes c(c - 1)/2 subarrays; the running version adds the same total one step at a time.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import Counter\n\n\ndef count_equal_keys(a, step, start=0):\n    \"\"\"Subarrays whose two bounding prefix keys are equal; key_(i+1) = step(key_i, a[i]).\"\"\"\n    seen = Counter({start: 1})\n    key = start\n    count = 0\n    for x in a:\n        key = step(key, x)\n        count += seen[key]\n        seen[key] += 1\n    return count\n\n\ndef count_xor_k(a, k):\n    \"\"\"Subarrays whose XOR is exactly k: look up cur ^ k instead of cur.\"\"\"\n    seen = Counter({0: 1})\n    cur = count = 0\n    for x in a:\n        cur ^= x\n        count += seen[cur ^ k]     # px[j] ^ px[i] == k  &lt;=&gt;  px[j] == px[i] ^ k\n        seen[cur] += 1\n    return count\n\n\n# Sums divisible by k: count_equal_keys(a, lambda s, x: (s + x) % k)\n# XOR equal to zero:   count_equal_keys(a, lambda s, x: s ^ x)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count the subarrays of \u003ccode>[2, -7, 4, 3, 5]\u003c/code> whose sum is divisible by 3.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Seed remainder 0 once (the empty prefix). \u003ccode>seen[r]\u003c/code> counts earlier prefixes with remainder r\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         2  -7   4   3   5\nseen by remainder  r0=1  r1=0  r2=0\ncount 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">prefix 2, remainder 2: no earlier prefix has remainder 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>  -7   4   3   5\nprefix    2\n% 3       \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\nseen by remainder  r0=1  r1=0  r2=1\ncount 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">prefix -5: Python gives \u003ccode>-5 % 3 = 1\u003c/code> (C++ and Java give -2). No earlier remainder 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         2  \u003cmark>-7\u003c/mark>   4   3   5\nprefix    2  -5\n% 3       2   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\nseen by remainder  r0=1  r1=1  r2=1\ncount 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">prefix -1, remainder 2: 1 earlier prefix shares it, so 1 new subarray ends here\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         2  -7   \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>   3   5\nprefix    2  -5  -1\n% 3       2   1   \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\nseen by remainder  r0=1  r1=1  r2=2\ncount 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">prefix 2, remainder 2: 2 earlier prefixes share it, so 2 new subarrays end here\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         2  -7   4   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   5\nprefix    2  -5  -1   2\n% 3       2   1   2   \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\nseen by remainder  r0=1  r1=1  r2=3\ncount 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">prefix 7, remainder 1: 1 earlier prefix shares it, so 1 new subarray ends here\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         2  -7   4   3   \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>\nprefix    2  -5  -1   2   7\n% 3       2   1   2   2   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\nseen by remainder  r0=1  r1=2  r2=3\ncount 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Total 4: \u003ccode>[-7, 4]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>[-7, 4, 3]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>[3]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>[4, 3, 5]\u003c/code>. The last one pairs prefixes -5 and 7, which C-style \u003ccode>%\u003c/code> would miss\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         2  -7   4   3   5\nprefix    2  -5  -1   2   7\n% 3       2   1   2   2   1\ncount 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>C-style remainders\u003c/b> in C++ and Java break the matching for negative prefixes (see the last step above).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>\"Length at least 2\"\u003c/b> needs positions, not counts: store the first index of each remainder and require \u003ccode>i - first &gt;= 2\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Seeds:\u003c/b> remainder 0 counted once for counts; balance 0 at index -1 for longest-length questions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>More than two symbols:\u003c/b> scoring 0 as -1 only works for two categories; for three, use a tuple of differences such as \u003ccode>(count_a - count_b, count_b - count_c)\u003c/code> as the key.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In C++, \u003ccode>-5 % 3\u003c/code> is -2. In the worked example, which subarray would a direct C++ translation miss, and how do you fix it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>[4, 3, 5]\u003c/code>: its bounding prefixes are -5 and 7, whose remainders are -2 and 1 in C++, so they do not match. Normalise with \u003ccode>((x % k) + k) % k\u003c/code>; Python's \u003ccode>%\u003c/code> already does.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the longest stretch of \u003ccode>[1, 1, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1]\u003c/code> with equally many 0s and 1s?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>6. Scoring 0 as -1 gives balances 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 0, 1. Balance 0 was first seen at the seed index -1 and again at index 5 (length 6), and balance 1 first at index 0 and again at index 6 (also length 6).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to rephrase divisibility, XOR and balance conditions as \"two prefixes share a key\" and count them in one pass.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:prefix/2d-prefix-sums-and-integral-images":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Let \u003ccode>P[r][c]\u003c/code> be the sum of everything above and to the left of the corner (r, c), with an extra row and column of zeros. A rectangle \u003ccode>[r1, r2) x [c1, c2)\u003c/code> is then \u003ccode>P[r2][c2] - P[r1][c2] - P[r2][c1] + P[r1][c1]\u003c/code>: take the big block, remove the strip above and the strip to the left, and add back the corner you removed twice. O(mn) to build, O(1) per query.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>           c1         c2\n     +----------+---------+\n     |    A     |    B    |     P[r1][c1] = A\n  r1 +----------+---------+     P[r1][c2] = A + B\n     |    C     |  query  |     P[r2][c1] = A + C\n  r2 +----------+---------+     P[r2][c2] = A + B + C + query\n\n  query = P[r2][c2] - P[r1][c2] - P[r2][c1] + P[r1][c1]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Build with the same idea:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>P[r+1][c+1] = g[r][c] + P[r][c+1] + P[r+1][c] - P[r][c]\u003c/code>, one O(1) step per cell.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Integral images:\u003c/b> computer vision calls this table an integral image. The Viola-Jones face detector evaluates each rectangular Haar feature with a few lookups, so a feature costs the same at every size and scale; a box blur costs the same per pixel at any radius. OpenCV provides it as \u003ccode>cv2.integral\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>NumPy:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>P[1:, 1:] = g.cumsum(axis=0, dtype=np.int64).cumsum(axis=1)\u003c/code> builds it in C. Choose the dtype deliberately: an all-white 5000 x 5000 image sums to 6.4 x 10^9, which wraps a uint32 table (the lab shows it).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fix a band, collapse to 1D:\u003c/b> to count sub-rectangles with sum t, loop over row pairs (top, bottom), keep running column sums for the band, and run the 1D prefix-map count on them. With R rows and C columns that is O(R^2 C), so put the smaller dimension on the outside.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Higher dimensions:\u003c/b> the same pattern uses 2^d corners with alternating signs.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def build_2d(grid):\n    \"\"\"P[r][c] = sum of grid[:r][:c] (the rectangle above and left of (r, c)).\"\"\"\n    m = len(grid)\n    n = len(grid[0]) if m else 0\n    P = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(m + 1)]\n    for r in range(m):\n        for c in range(n):\n            P[r + 1][c + 1] = grid[r][c] + P[r][c + 1] + P[r + 1][c] - P[r][c]\n    return P\n\n\ndef rect_sum(P, r1, c1, r2, c2):\n    \"\"\"Sum of grid[r1:r2][c1:c2] (half-open in both directions), O(1).\"\"\"\n    return P[r2][c2] - P[r1][c2] - P[r2][c1] + P[r1][c1]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The grid and the query: rows 1 to 2, columns 1 to 2, half-open \u003ccode>[1, 3) x [1, 3)\u003c/code>\u003c/p>\u003cpre>grid\n   3   0   1   4\n   5   \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   2\n   1   \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>   1\n   4   1   0   5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Build \u003ccode>P\u003c/code> (one extra zero row and column). For example \u003ccode>P[2][2] = g[1][1] + P[1][2] + P[2][1] - P[1][1] = 6 + 3 + 8 - 3 = 14\u003c/code>\u003c/p>\u003cpre>P\n   0   0   0   0   0\n   0   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   4   8\n   0   \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>14\u003c/mark>  18  24\n   0   9  17  21  28\n   0  13  22  26  38\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Query: start from \u003ccode>P[3][3] = 21\u003c/code>, everything above and left of the bottom-right corner\u003c/p>\u003cpre>P\n   0   0   0   0   0\n   0   3   3   4   8\n   0   8  14  18  24\n   0   9  17  \u003cmark>21\u003c/mark>  28\n   0  13  22  26  38\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Subtract \u003ccode>P[1][3] = 4\u003c/code> (rows above the box) and \u003ccode>P[3][1] = 9\u003c/code> (columns left of it)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>P\n   0   0   0   0   0\n   0   3   3   \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>   8\n   0   8  14  18  24\n   0   \u003cmark>9\u003c/mark>  17  21  28\n   0  13  22  26  38\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The top-left block \u003ccode>P[1][1] = 3\u003c/code> was subtracted twice, so add it back: 21 - 4 - 9 + 3 = 11 = 6 + 3 + 2 + 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>P\n   0   0   0   0   0\n   0   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   3   4   8\n   0   8  14  18  24\n   0   9  17  21  28\n   0  13  22  26  38\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Inclusive corners:\u003c/b> for an inclusive rectangle (r1, c1) to (r2, c2), shift the far corner: \u003ccode>P[r2+1][c2+1] - P[r1][c2+1] - P[r2+1][c1] + P[r1][c1]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Aliased rows:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>[[0] * (n + 1)] * (m + 1)\u003c/code> makes every row the same list; build rows with a comprehension.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fixed-width overflow\u003c/b> in NumPy, C++ and Java tables.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Empty grids:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>len(grid[0])\u003c/code> raises on \u003ccode>[]\u003c/code>; guard it as the template does.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Using the table \u003ccode>P\u003c/code> in the trace, what is the sum of the bottom row of the grid?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It is the rectangle \u003ccode>[3, 4) x [0, 4)\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>P[4][4] - P[3][4] - P[4][0] + P[3][0] = 38 - 28 - 0 + 0 = 10\u003c/code>, which matches 4 + 1 + 0 + 5.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to read any rectangle sum from four corners of an integral image, and how to collapse a 2D counting problem to 1D by fixing a band of rows.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:prefix/difference-arrays-many-range-updates-one-rebuild":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>The difference array \u003ccode>d[i] = a[i] - a[i - 1]\u003c/code> is the inverse of the prefix sum: a running sum of \u003ccode>d\u003c/code> gives \u003ccode>a\u003c/code> back. Adding v to the whole slice \u003ccode>a[l:r]\u003c/code> changes only two differences, \u003ccode>d[l] += v\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>d[r] -= v\u003c/code>. So record each update in O(1) and rebuild once at the end in O(n): O(n + q) instead of O(nq).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Inverse operations:\u003c/b> prefix sums and differences undo each other, like integrals and derivatives. A range addition is a step up at l and a step down at r.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Spare slot:\u003c/b> keep \u003ccode>d\u003c/code> one longer than \u003ccode>a\u003c/code> so an update ending at \u003ccode>r == n\u003c/code> needs no bounds check.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Starting from a non-zero array:\u003c/b> either start \u003ccode>d\u003c/code> from the differences of \u003ccode>a\u003c/code>, or rebuild the additions from zeros and add them to \u003ccode>a\u003c/code> element by element.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Sweep-line view:\u003c/b> +v at each start and -v at each end, processed in position order, tracks \"how many are active right now\" (passengers in a car, meetings in progress, flips covering this bit). You can consume \u003ccode>d\u003c/code> while still writing it, left to right, as long as every new update starts at or after the current position.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>2D:\u003c/b> for \u003ccode>[r1, r2) x [c1, c2)\u003c/code> add +v at (r1, c1), -v at (r1, c2), -v at (r2, c1) and +v at (r2, c2), then take running sums along rows and then down columns.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Huge coordinates:\u003c/b> if positions reach 10^9, sort the start and end events (or compress the coordinates) instead of allocating an array.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The limit:\u003c/b> a read between updates needs a rebuild each time. Interleaved range updates and point reads call for a Fenwick tree over \u003ccode>d\u003c/code> (the range-query module).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from itertools import accumulate\n\n\ndef apply_range_adds(n, updates):\n    \"\"\"Each (l, r, v) adds v to a[l:r] (half-open); returns the final array, O(n + len(updates)).\"\"\"\n    d = [0] * (n + 1)              # one spare slot so r == n needs no check\n    for l, r, v in updates:\n        d[l] += v\n        d[r] -= v\n    return list(accumulate(d[:n]))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Three updates on six zeros: +2 on \u003ccode>a[1:4]\u003c/code>, +3 on \u003ccode>a[2:6]\u003c/code> and -1 on \u003ccode>a[0:2]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start with \u003ccode>d = [0] * (n + 1)\u003c/code>; slot 6 is the spare that lets \u003ccode>r == n\u003c/code> work without a check\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0   1   2   3   4   5   6\nd         0   0   0   0   0   0   0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add +2 to \u003ccode>a[1:4]\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>d[1] += 2\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>d[4] -= 2\u003c/code>. Two writes, however long the range\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0   1   2   3   4   5   6\nd         0   \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>   0   0  \u003cmark>-2\u003c/mark>   0   0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add +3 to \u003ccode>a[2:6]\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>d[2] += 3\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>d[6] -= 3\u003c/code>. Two writes, however long the range\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0   1   2   3   4   5   6\nd         0   2   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   0  -2   0  \u003cmark>-3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add -1 to \u003ccode>a[0:2]\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>d[0] -= 1\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>d[2] += 1\u003c/code>. Two writes, however long the range\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0   1   2   3   4   5   6\nd        \u003cmark>-1\u003c/mark>   2   \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>   0  -2   0  -3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Rebuild with one running sum over \u003ccode>d[:6]\u003c/code>: each value is the total of every start minus every stop so far\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0   1   2   3   4   5   6\nd        -1   2   4   0  -2   0  -3\na        \u003cmark>-1\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Check one cell: \u003ccode>a[2]\u003c/code> is inside \u003ccode>[1, 4)\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>[2, 6)\u003c/code> but not \u003ccode>[0, 2)\u003c/code>, so 2 + 3 = 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0   1   2   3   4   5\na        -1   1   \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>   5   3   3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Inclusive, 1-indexed inputs:\u003c/b> for \u003ccode>[first, last]\u003c/code>, write \u003ccode>d[first - 1] += v\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>d[last] -= v\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reading \u003ccode>d[i]\u003c/code> as a value\u003c/b> before the rebuild.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The 2D sign pattern:\u003c/b> two minus corners, two plus corners; a wrong sign leaks the update across the grid.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Allocating 10^9 slots\u003c/b> when only a few thousand positions matter.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Five counters start at 0. Add 5 to positions 1 to 3, 10 to positions 2 to 4 and 7 to positions 4 to 5 (1-indexed, inclusive). What is \u003ccode>d\u003c/code> before the rebuild, and what are the final values?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The 0-indexed half-open updates are \u003ccode>(0, 3, 5)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>(1, 4, 10)\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>(3, 5, 7)\u003c/code>, so \u003ccode>d = [5, 10, 0, 2, -10, -7]\u003c/code> and the running sum gives \u003ccode>[5, 15, 15, 17, 7]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to batch any number of range additions into two writes each and recover every value with one running sum.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:prefix/left-and-right-passes":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>When the answer at index i combines everything to its left with everything to its right, compute \u003ccode>left[i]\u003c/code> (an aggregate of \u003ccode>a[:i]\u003c/code>) in a forward pass and \u003ccode>right[i]\u003c/code> (of \u003ccode>a[i+1:]\u003c/code>) in a backward pass, then combine them per index. Any associative operation with an identity works: sum, product, max, min, gcd. A close cousin keeps a running extreme of the prefix sums themselves.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Exclusive scans\u003c/b> generalise prefix sums to any associative operation; \"exclusive\" means \u003ccode>a[i]\u003c/code> itself is left out of both sides.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Why not subtract from the total:\u003c/b> subtraction works for sums (the right side of i is \u003ccode>total - left - a[i]\u003c/code>) but there is no inverse for max or min, and dividing a product by \u003ccode>a[i]\u003c/code> fails when \u003ccode>a[i] == 0\u003c/code>. Two passes need no inverse.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>O(1) extra space:\u003c/b> write \u003ccode>left\u003c/code> straight into the output array, then sweep backwards multiplying by a running right aggregate.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Running extremes of prefixes:\u003c/b> the best subarray sum ending at i is \u003ccode>pre[i + 1] - min(pre[0..i])\u003c/code>; the largest absolute subarray sum is \u003ccode>max(pre) - min(pre)\u003c/code> over all prefixes (the empty one included), because the absolute value makes their order irrelevant.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Best on each side:\u003c/b> to choose non-overlapping windows, precompute \u003ccode>best_left[i]\u003c/code> (the best window ending at or before i) and \u003ccode>best_right[i]\u003c/code> (the best starting at or after i), then try every middle choice and combine in O(1). When the earliest indices must win ties, use \u003ccode>&gt;\u003c/code> in the forward pass and \u003ccode>&gt;=\u003c/code> in the backward one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Elsewhere in the route:\u003c/b> trapping rain water uses left and right maxima (two pointers module), and next-greater problems refine \"everything to my right\" with a monotonic stack.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def exclusive_scans(a, op, identity):\n    \"\"\"left[i] = op over a[:i] and right[i] = op over a[i+1:], both O(n).\"\"\"\n    n = len(a)\n    left, right = [identity] * n, [identity] * n\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        left[i] = op(left[i - 1], a[i - 1])\n    for i in range(n - 2, -1, -1):\n        right[i] = op(right[i + 1], a[i + 1])\n    return left, right\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Left and right maxima of \u003ccode>[2, 7, 1, 8, 2, 8]\u003c/code>, using \u003ccode>max\u003c/code> with identity \u003ccode>-inf\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Left pass: \u003ccode>left[i] = max(left[i - 1], a[i - 1])\u003c/code>, the largest value strictly before i (identity -inf at i = 0)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a          2    7    1    8    2    8\nleft    \u003cmark>-inf\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Fill left to right; each entry costs one comparison\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a          2    7    1    8    2    8\nleft    -inf    \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Right pass from the end: \u003ccode>right[i] = max(right[i + 1], a[i + 1])\u003c/code>, the largest value strictly after i\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a          2    7    1    8    2    8\nleft    -inf    2    7    7    8    8\nright      \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark> \u003cmark>-inf\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Combine per index in O(1): \u003ccode>a[i] &gt; left[i]\u003c/code> marks a new record, at indices [0, 1, 3]; swap max for multiplication and \u003ccode>left[i] * right[i]\u003c/code> is the product of everything except \u003ccode>a[i]\u003c/code>\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a          \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>    1    \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>    2    8\nleft    -inf    2    7    7    8    8\nright      8    8    8    8    8 -inf\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Wrong identity:\u003c/b> 0 is not the identity for max over negative numbers; use \u003ccode>float(\"-inf\")\u003c/code> (or 1 for products, 0 for sums and gcd).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Inclusive versus exclusive\u003c/b> scans differ by one position; decide whether \u003ccode>a[i]\u003c/code> belongs on a side.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ties\u003c/b> in best-left and best-right arrays when the smallest indices must win.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Division shortcuts\u003c/b> that break on zeros.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Run the two passes with multiplication on \u003ccode>[3, 1, 2, 5]\u003c/code>. What are \u003ccode>left\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>right\u003c/code> and the product of everything except each element?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>left = [1, 3, 3, 6]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>right = [10, 10, 5, 1]\u003c/code>, and the products are \u003ccode>[10, 30, 15, 6]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to answer \"everything except me\" and \"best on each side\" questions with one pass from each end and an O(1) combine per index.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","lab:prefix/integral-image-region-sums-and-a-box-blur-at-any":"\u003cp>You will compute region sums over a 2000 x 2000 image naively and with an integral image, check that both agree on 10,000 random rectangles, apply a box blur at four radii both ways, and watch a too-narrow integer type wrap.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.10+ with \u003ccode>pip install numpy\u003c/code>; about 1 GB of free memory.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Save this as \u003ccode>integral_lab.py\u003c/code> and run it with \u003ccode>python integral_lab.py\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import time\nimport numpy as np\n\nH, W = 2000, 2000\nrng = np.random.default_rng(1)\nimg = rng.integers(0, 256, size=(H, W), dtype=np.uint8)\n\n\ndef integral(img):\n    \"\"\"P[r, c] = img[:r, :c].sum(); one extra row and column of zeros.\"\"\"\n    P = np.zeros((img.shape[0] + 1, img.shape[1] + 1), dtype=np.int64)\n    P[1:, 1:] = img.cumsum(axis=0, dtype=np.int64).cumsum(axis=1)\n    return P\n\n\ndef region_sum(P, r1, c1, r2, c2):\n    \"\"\"img[r1:r2, c1:c2].sum() in four lookups.\"\"\"\n    return int(P[r2, c2] - P[r1, c2] - P[r2, c1] + P[r1, c1])\n\n\nt = time.perf_counter()\nP = integral(img)\nprint(f\"build integral image: {(time.perf_counter() - t) * 1000:.1f} ms\")\n\nrects = []\nfor _ in range(10_000):\n    r1, r2 = sorted(rng.integers(0, H + 1, size=2))\n    c1, c2 = sorted(rng.integers(0, W + 1, size=2))\n    rects.append((int(r1), int(c1), int(r2), int(c2)))\n\nt = time.perf_counter()\nnaive = [int(img[r1:r2, c1:c2].sum(dtype=np.int64)) for r1, c1, r2, c2 in rects]\nnaive_s = time.perf_counter() - t\nt = time.perf_counter()\nfast = [region_sum(P, *q) for q in rects]\nfast_s = time.perf_counter() - t\nassert naive == fast\nprint(f\"10,000 rectangles: naive {naive_s:.2f} s, integral {fast_s * 1000:.1f} ms, \"\n      f\"{naive_s / fast_s:.0f}x faster, all equal\")\n\n\ndef box_blur_shifts(img, r):\n    \"\"\"Mean over a (2r+1) x (2r+1) window by adding shifted copies: (2r+1)^2 whole-image adds.\"\"\"\n    pad = np.pad(img.astype(np.int64), r, mode=\"edge\")\n    acc = np.zeros(img.shape, dtype=np.int64)\n    for dy in range(2 * r + 1):\n        for dx in range(2 * r + 1):\n            acc += pad[dy:dy + img.shape[0], dx:dx + img.shape[1]]\n    return acc // (2 * r + 1) ** 2\n\n\ndef box_blur_integral(img, r):\n    \"\"\"The same blur with four whole-array lookups into the integral image of the padded image.\"\"\"\n    pad = np.pad(img, r, mode=\"edge\")\n    P = integral(pad)\n    k = 2 * r + 1\n    h, w = img.shape\n    s = P[k:k + h, k:k + w] - P[0:h, k:k + w] - P[k:k + h, 0:w] + P[0:h, 0:w]\n    return s // (k * k)\n\n\nfor r in (1, 3, 7, 15):\n    t = time.perf_counter(); a = box_blur_shifts(img, r); ts = time.perf_counter() - t\n    t = time.perf_counter(); b = box_blur_integral(img, r); ti = time.perf_counter() - t\n    assert np.array_equal(a, b)\n    print(f\"box blur r={r:&gt;2}: shifted adds {ts * 1000:7.0f} ms, integral image {ti * 1000:5.0f} ms\")\n\nwhite = np.full((5000, 5000), 255, dtype=np.uint8)\nP32 = np.zeros((5001, 5001), dtype=np.uint32)\nP32[1:, 1:] = white.cumsum(axis=0, dtype=np.uint32).cumsum(axis=1, dtype=np.uint32)\nprint(\"uint32 corner:\", int(P32[-1, -1]), \" true total:\", 255 * 5000 * 5000)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Before you read the output, predict: how many lookups does one region sum take, and how many whole-image additions does the shifted blur do at r = 15?\u003c/li>\u003cli>Draw the four corners used by \u003ccode>box_blur_integral\u003c/code> for one pixel on paper and check them against \u003ccode>rect_sum\u003c/code> from the 2D recap: the slices are that formula applied to every pixel at once.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Change \u003ccode>rects\u003c/code> to only tiny rectangles (side at most 5). Does the naive approach catch up? Explain why from what each method touches.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Fix the overflow line by choosing a dtype that cannot wrap, and state the largest image of 8-bit pixels that a uint32 table can hold safely (255 x H x W must stay below 2^32).\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cp>On a recent laptop or server, single-threaded:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>Building the integral image takes roughly 100 to 250 ms; it is two passes of C loops over 4 million pixels.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The naive region sums take several seconds for 10,000 random rectangles (the average rectangle covers about a ninth of the image, roughly 440,000 pixels), while the integral image answers all of them in a few tens of milliseconds: a few hundred times faster, and every answer equal.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The shifted-copy blur grows with (2r + 1)^2: roughly 0.1 to 0.2 s at r = 1, 2 to 2.5 s at r = 7 and around 10 s at r = 15. The integral blur stays near 0.1 to 0.25 s at every radius, because it always does four lookups per pixel. At r = 1 the two are about level, or the shifted version wins: nine whole-image additions are cheap, and the integral version also pays to build its table.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With tiny rectangles the naive sums drop to tens of milliseconds for all 10,000 and the gap shrinks to a few times: each call's Python and NumPy overhead now costs more than the arithmetic. The table's advantage is that its cost never depends on the area.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The uint32 corner prints 2080032704 instead of 6375000000: the true total wrapped modulo 2^32 without any error. A uint32 table is only safe while 255 x H x W &lt; 2^32, about 16.8 million pixels (for example 4096 x 4096).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>","p:303":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Many sum queries over different ranges of an array that never changes after construction.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Doing the work per query costs O(n) each time. What could you compute once so that each query becomes a couple of lookups?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix sum array\u003c/p>\u003cp>Store pre with pre[0] = 0 and pre[i + 1] = pre[i] + nums[i]; the inclusive range left..right is pre[right + 1] - pre[left]. Building is O(n) and every query is two lookups and one subtraction.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) build, O(1) per query, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:724":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Find an index where the total on its left equals the total on its right.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>If you know the total of the whole array and the sum to the left of i, what is the sum to the right of i?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix and suffix passes\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep a running left sum; index i balances when left == total - left - nums[i]. Test before adding nums[i] to left and return the first index that balances, or -1. One pass after computing the total.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:560":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count contiguous subarrays with an exact sum, and the values may be negative.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A window cannot decide when to shrink once values can be negative. Write the sum of nums[j:i] as a difference of two prefix sums: which earlier value are you looking for at step i?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix sums with a hash map\u003c/p>\u003cp>nums[j:i] sums to k exactly when pre[j] = pre[i] - k, so keep a Counter of earlier prefixes seeded with {0: 1}; at each step add seen[cur - k], then record cur. Looking up before recording keeps every counted subarray non-empty.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:974":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count subarrays whose sum is a multiple of k, with negative values allowed.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When is pre[i] - pre[j] a multiple of k? Compare the two prefixes modulo k.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix remainders, XOR and balance\u003c/p>\u003cp>A difference is divisible by k exactly when both prefixes leave the same remainder, so count remainders in a list of size k (remainder 0 seeded once) and add the count of the current remainder before incrementing it. Python's % already returns 0..k-1 for negative sums; in C++ or Java normalise with ((x % k) + k) % k.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n + k) time, O(k) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:525":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The longest stretch of a binary array with as many 0s as 1s.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Can you rewrite \"equal counts\" as \"a sum equals something\" by changing how you score each element?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix remainders, XOR and balance\u003c/p>\u003cp>Score 1 as +1 and 0 as -1; a stretch is balanced when its sum is 0, that is when two prefix balances are equal. Store the first index of each balance (seed balance 0 at index -1) and maximise i - first[balance]; never overwrite a first index.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:304":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Many rectangle-sum queries on a matrix that does not change.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Extend the 1D idea: what single number per corner would let you peel any rectangle out of a few stored values?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> 2D prefix sums\u003c/p>\u003cp>Let P[r][c] be the sum of everything above and to the left of corner (r, c) in an (m + 1) x (n + 1) table, built with P[r+1][c+1] = cell + P[r][c+1] + P[r+1][c] - P[r][c]. An inclusive rectangle (r1, c1)..(r2, c2) is P[r2+1][c2+1] - P[r1][c2+1] - P[r2+1][c1] + P[r1][c1].\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(mn) build, O(1) per query, O(mn) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1109":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Many bookings that each add seats to every flight from first to last, and you only need the final totals.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Touching every flight of every booking is O(n) per booking. Record only where each booking starts and where it stops.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Difference array\u003c/p>\u003cp>Difference array: d[first - 1] += seats and d[last] -= seats (the input is 1-indexed and inclusive), then one running sum over d gives every flight's total. The final pass costs O(n) however long the bookings are.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n + b) time for b bookings, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1749":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The largest absolute value of any subarray sum.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every subarray sum is a difference of two prefix sums. Which pair of prefixes gives the largest absolute difference?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix and suffix passes\u003c/p>\u003cp>|pre[i] - pre[j]| is largest for the largest and the smallest prefix, the empty prefix 0 included, and with an absolute value their order does not matter. So the answer is max(pre) - min(pre), tracked in one pass with two running extremes.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1074":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count all submatrices whose sum equals a target, in a grid of up to about 100 x 100.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Fix the top and bottom rows of the submatrix. What 1D problem is left?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> 2D prefix sums\u003c/p>\u003cp>For each pair of rows (top, bottom), keep running column sums for the band; the submatrices inside that band with sum target are exactly the subarrays of the column-sum array with sum target, which the prefix-map count finds in O(columns). Put the smaller dimension in the outer pair loop.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(min(m, n)^2 x max(m, n)) time, O(max(m, n)) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:2536":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Add 1 to many sub-rectangles of an n x n grid, then return the whole grid.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The 1D trick marks a start and a stop. What marks does a rectangle need so that a 2D running sum recreates it?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Difference array\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use a 2D difference array: +1 at (r1, c1), -1 at (r1, c2 + 1), -1 at (r2 + 1, c1) and +1 at (r2 + 1, c2 + 1) in an (n + 1) x (n + 1) table, then take running sums along each row and then down each column.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(q + n^2) time, O(n^2) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:995":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Flip windows of exactly k consecutive bits to turn the whole array into 1s, with as few flips as possible.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Look at the leftmost 0. Which flips can still change it, and is there any choice about using one?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Difference array\u003c/p>\u003cp>Scan left to right keeping the number of active flips with a difference array (+1 where a flip starts, -1 at start + k). If the bit at i is 0 after an even number of flips (or 1 after an odd number), a flip must start at i, since no later flip covers i; if i + k &gt; n, return -1. Each step is O(1).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space (O(1) with in-place markers)\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:689":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Choose three non-overlapping length-k subarrays with the largest total, preferring the smallest starting indices on ties.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Fix the middle window. What do you need to know about the best window entirely to its left and the best entirely to its right?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix and suffix passes\u003c/p>\u003cp>Compute every window sum from prefix sums, then best_left[i] (index of the best window starting at or before i, keeping the earlier one on ties with &gt;) and best_right[i] (best starting at or after i, preferring the earlier one with &gt;= while scanning right to left). Try every middle start and combine three O(1) lookups.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>"}]);
